    {
     "@type": "Question",
     "name": "Why are my snake plant leaves turning yellow and soft, and how do I save it from root rot?",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "Yellow, soft or mushy snake plant leaves almost always mean overwatering and root rot. The soil stays wet, and the roots suffocate and begin to rot from the base upward."
     }
    },
    {
     "@type": "Question",
     "name": "Why are my snake plant leaf tips turning brown?",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "Brown, dry tips on a snake plant usually come from salt and mineral build-up in the soil, from the minerals in tap water or from too much fertiliser. The salts stay behind in the pot as the water dries out."
     }
    },
    {
     "@type": "Question",
     "name": "Why are my snake plant leaves wrinkled or curling?",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "Wrinkled, thin or curling leaves mean the snake plant is not getting enough water. Either the soil has been bone dry for too long, or rotten roots can no longer take water up."
     }
    },
    {
     "@type": "Question",
     "name": "Why is my snake plant falling over?",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "Snake plant leaves that bend, flop or fall outward are weak from overwatering or from too little light, which makes them grow tall and thin. A top-heavy plant in a light plastic pot can also tip over."
     }
    },
    {
     "@type": "Question",
     "name": "What are the pale or brown dry patches on my snake plant?",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "Pale, bleached or brown papery patches on a snake plant are sunburn. This is common in the UAE when a plant sits in afternoon sun behind glass, or outdoors in summer without shade."
     }
    },
    {
     "@type": "Question",
     "name": "What is the white cotton at the base of my snake plant leaves?",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "White, cottony clusters between snake plant leaves are mealybugs, a sap-sucking pest. They hide deep in the leaf base and spread to nearby plants."
     }
    },
    {
     "@type": "Question",
     "name": "Why are there tiny flies around my snake plant?",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "Small black flies around snake plant soil are fungus gnats. They breed in soil that stays wet, so they are a sign of overwatering."
     }
    },
    {
     "@type": "Question",
     "name": "Why is my snake plant not growing?",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "Snake plants are naturally slow, and in low light or in winter they can stop growing for months. That is normal and not a sign of a problem as long as the leaves stay firm and green."
